# Vallaki's townsfolk about their day (lane 4, F2): Goodwife Marta on her morning round of the market row and the main
# street, Old Kolya hauling festival wicker from the lower town, and Ilie lighting the lamps after dark. They walk
# their routes (the location's `path`) and stop when someone comes up to them.

~ goodwife
if flag.festival_over and flag.vallaki_backed == "neither"
Goodwife Marta [smile]: No festival this week. Nobody's said we may stop smiling, but nobody's checking either. I'd forgotten what my own face does.
-> END
endif
if flag.festival_begun and not flag.festival_over
Goodwife Marta: Off to the square with you. They count the faces, you know. Mine's been counted. I'm only fetching bread.
-> END
endif
Goodwife Marta [smile]: Good morning! It is a good morning. The Baron says so, and the Baron's men are standing right over there, so it is.
* How is the market? -> market
* Good morning to you. -> END

~ market
Goodwife Marta: Turnips, mostly. Turnips and Blinsky's toys, which nobody buys, and the Baron's festival bunting, which everybody buys, because it's noticed who doesn't.
interject tag:blunt: So the bunting's a tax.
Goodwife Marta: Hush. It's a celebration.
-> END

~ carter
if flag.festival_over
Old Kolya: No more wicker to haul. My back thanks you, whoever you are. My purse doesn't.
-> END
endif
Old Kolya: Mind the cart. That's a sun, that is. Will be. Every week I bring the wicker up, every week they burn it, every week the sky stays grey.
Old Kolya: Don't tell the Baron I said "grey".
-> END

~ lamplighter
Ilie the Lamplighter: Evening. Every lamp lit by the last bell, or I answer for it. The Baron says a lit street is a happy street.
if guest:ireena
Ilie the Lamplighter: Your friend should keep her hood up. The dark has eyes on this town, and some of them are at the windows.
endif
-> END
